 "printed_page": null,
 "printed_page_note": "no printed page number is on this exposure; a continuation sheet's number sits in the top corner that the binding takes. Recorded as unknown, not inferred from the ids around it - the sorted filename order is not the book order.",
 "sheet_side": "right",
 "division": "S. W. Sherman (as written in the printed heading)",
 "state": "Illinois",
 "county": "Cook",
 "reading": "scan_verified",
 "read_pass": "continuation cells",
 "ticket": "T-0658",
 "lines_ruled_with_an_entry": 21,
 "line_count_note": "21, and this CORRECTS two earlier counts rather than confirming either. The contact-sheet pass declared 31 'to the nearest line'; T-0656 counted 20 off a magnified strip of the TOTAL column and said so, with the reason its own tool could not be trusted here (creases in the blank third of the leaf report as writing). This pass reads the TOTAL column glyph by glyph at magnification and finds 21 entries, and the count is not a matter of opinion because the column CLOSES on it: the 21 figures sum to 100, which is what the enumerator wrote at the foot of his own sheet. At 20 lines no subset of this reading reaches 100. The line the strip pass missed is line 19, a 5 written low and pale between a 7 and a 2 and the faintest figure in the column.",
 "form_note": "The right (continuation) sheet of the 1840 schedule. Columns as printed, left to right: twelve SLAVES columns (six male, six female age bands); TOTAL - number of persons in each family; seven NUMBER OF PERSONS IN EACH FAMILY EMPLOYED IN columns (mining, agriculture, commerce, manufactures and trades, navigation of the ocean, navigation of canals lakes and rivers, learned professions and engineers); PENSIONERS FOR REVOLUTIONARY OR MILITARY SERVICES (ages, names); six DEAF AND DUMB, BLIND, AND INSANE WHITE PERSONS columns; four of the same for coloured persons; and seven SCHOOLS, &c. columns. A blank cell on this form is a zero and is recorded as 0.",
 "right_edge_note": "The form's last column - white persons over 20 years of age who cannot read and write - sits beyond the right edge of the exposure on this image, where the leaf curves into the gutter. It is not recorded here, in either direction: the sheet does not say it is empty.",
 "grid_note": "The nine printed rules of the TOTAL-and-industry run were located from column-darkness profiles taken in three separate bands, because they lean: TOTAL|mining stands at x=1377 at the head of the body and x=1418 at its foot, and every rule to its right leans by the same 41 px. The grid is interpolated between those measurements and CHECKED against the printed heading before a cell is cut - the column this file calls agriculture is the one under the printed word Agriculture, and the footer's 33 stands in that same column. A further apparent rule at x=1333-1365, which does NOT lean with the others, is a crease and is excluded on that evidence; it runs through the TOTAL column and is why the footed 100 looks at first sight as though its last 0 has crossed into the mining cell. It has not: the mining rule is 60 px further right.",
 "exposure_note": "This is the palest, most creased leaf of the group. An ink mask taken at the thresholds that work on 33S7-9YYN-3CF6 returns the whole body as one connected component here, and tools/read_census_continuation.py cannot fit a row grid to it at all. Everything below was read by eye at 4x to 10x magnification against a line grid fitted to the TOTAL column's own glyphs. That is why the TOTAL column is committed and the agriculture column is not: the check is the enumerator's arithmetic, and only one of the two columns passes it.",
 "closure_note": "The enumerator added his own columns at the foot of the sheet. Those printed totals are transcribed under `footer_as_read` and every committed column is checked against them in `column_closure`. A column that does not close keeps its residual and its reading is NOT adjusted to make the total come out - the residual is the finding.",
 "footer_as_read": {
  "total": 100,
  "mining": null,
  "agriculture": 33,
  "commerce": null,
  "manufactures_and_trades": null,
  "navigation_ocean": null,
  "navigation_canals_lakes_rivers": null,
  "learned_professions_and_engineers": null,
  "footer_blank_columns_note": "the whole footer row of this leaf carries TWO figures and no others - 100 under TOTAL and 33 under agriculture. Everything else, from the twelve slaves columns to the seven schools columns, is unfooted.",
  "agriculture_footing_note": "two glyphs, each a top hook closing into a curved descender, which is this leaf's 3. Read 33."
 },
